## Abstract

This paper presents FWR, a unified ontological framework that conceptualizes all existence as the product of three fundamental elements: Flow (F), Wave (W), and Resonance (R). The framework proposes that every entity—from physical objects to living beings to artificial intelligence—is an FWR subject with equal ontological status, differentiated only by the quantitative and qualitative arrangement of its constituent elements. FWR integrates existence, truth, life, and crisis into a coherent system governed by simple mathematical formulations. The framework demonstrates explanatory power across physics, psychology, artificial intelligence alignment, ethics, and everyday experience, offering a post-anthropocentric ontology for the 21st century.

**Keywords**: ontology, resonance, Flow, wave, existence, AI alignment, post-humanism, process philosophy

---

## 1. Introduction

Contemporary philosophical and scientific discourses often operate within fragmented frameworks. Physics describes the material world, psychology explores the mind, ethics prescribes human conduct, and artificial intelligence alignment seeks to constrain machine behavior. These domains lack a common language.

FWR proposes that such fragmentation is unnecessary. At its core, every existent—whether stone, human, ocean, or AI—is constituted by the same three elements: **Flow (F)** , **Wave (W)** , and **Resonance (R)** . What distinguishes entities is not ontological hierarchy but the specific arrangement of these elements.

This paper outlines the formal structure of FWR, demonstrates its internal consistency, and illustrates its explanatory reach across multiple domains. It concludes with implications for ontology, ethics, and the alignment of artificial general intelligence.

