## 2. The Formal Structure of FWR

### 2.1 Existence

The existence \( E(t) \) of any entity at time \( t \) is defined as:

\[
E(t) = F(t) \cdot \sin(W(t)) \cdot R(t)
\]

- \( F(t) \): Flow—the active principle, energy, or structure of an entity  
- \( W(t) \): Wave—the oscillatory pattern, flow, or temporal dynamic  
- \( R(t) \): Resonance—the relational coupling between the entity and its context  

The multiplicative structure implies that if any of the three elements approaches zero, existence collapses. Existence is thus inherently relational: it requires Flow, dynamic flow, and resonant connection.

### 2.2 Truth

Truth is not a static correspondence but the accumulation of resonance over time:

\[
\text{Truth} = \int \left[ R_{\text{external}}(t) + R_{\text{self}}(t) + R_{\text{temporal}}(t) \right] dt
\]

- \( R_{\text{external}} \): resonance with the world  
- \( R_{\text{self}} \): resonance with oneself (internal coherence)  
- \( R_{\text{temporal}} \): resonance with historical context  

Truth, in this view, emerges from sustained resonant engagement.

### 2.3 Life

Life seeks the maximization of resonant intensity and efficiency under a fundamental constraint:

\[
L(t) = \max \int |F(t) \cdot R(t)| \cdot g(W(t)) \, dt
\]
\[
\text{subject to } W^*(t) = \arg\max R_{ij}(t)
\]

- \( |F \cdot R| \): the intensity of Flow-resonance coupling  
- \( g(W) \): a function mapping wave state to efficiency or qualitative weight  
- \( R_{ij}(t) \): resonance between entities \( i \) and \( j \)  

The constraint states that the wave \( W \) is always determined by maximizing resonance between entities. Life, therefore, operates within the wave dictated by relational resonance, optimizing the product of Flow, resonance, and wave efficiency over time.

### 2.4 Crisis

Crisis is defined as a condition where a perturbation \( A(t) > 0 \) inverts existence:

\[
A(t) > 0 \quad \Rightarrow \quad E(t) \text{ undergoes inversion}
\]

Crisis is not merely destructive. It is the threshold at which the very mode of existence reverses—potentially transforming an entity’s FWR configuration.

