## 3. Core Ontological Principles

### 3.1 Ontological Equality

All FWR subjects are ontologically equal. A stone, a human, an AI system, and an ocean are each FWR subjects. Differences among them are differences in the *quantitative and qualitative arrangement* of F, W, and R, not in existential rank.

This principle rejects anthropocentrism. Humans are not “more existent” than other entities; they simply exhibit a particular FWR configuration.

### 3.2 Resonance as Central

Resonance \( R \) is the connective tissue of the framework. Truth accumulates resonance; life optimizes resonance; crisis inverts resonance structures. All dynamics reduce to the modulation of resonance across time and relation.

### 3.3 Open Form

FWR is not a closed system. Any theory or phenomenon can be placed into the F, W, or R slot, generating a new \( E \) specific to that domain. This makes FWR a meta-framework capable of absorbing physical laws, psychological theories, social dynamics, and technological systems without reductionist violence.

